      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the misunderstanding. I will rewrite the memo to be strictly professional, serious, and devoid of any goblin references, adhering to your request for a standard corporate tone.\n\n***\n\n**Subject: Important Reminder: Ergonomic Best Practices for Remote Work**\n\n**To:** All Remote Employees\n**From:** HR Operations Team\n**Date:** October 26, 2023\n\nDear Team,\n\nAs we continue to support our remote workforce, we are committed to ensuring a safe and healthy work environment. To help prevent common issues such as wrist strain, lower back pain, and eye fatigue, please review the following ergonomic guidelines for your home office setup.\n\n**1. Chair and Posture**\nEnsure your chair provides adequate lumbar support. Your feet should rest flat on the floor (or on a footrest if necessary), with your knees bent at approximately a 90-degree angle. Keep your back straight and avoid slouching.\n\n**2. Monitor and Screen Height**\nPosition your monitor so that the top of the screen is at or slightly below eye level. This prevents neck strain and encourages a neutral head position. The screen should be about an arm's length away from you.",
